  "notes": [
    "`time` read in seconds, from `GPS Time of Week`: it counts milliseconds, below a week's 604,800,000. It climbs by a median of 1000.0 per unit of its `time`, a millisecond's per second",
    "`pressure` read in pascals, from the values: its first value that is a launch site's pressure, 98358, is one in pascals. A launch site's pressure is 50,000 to 110,000 Pa, in pascals, kilopascals or inches of mercury",
    "`Pressure MSL` and `Pressure AGL` read in meters, from the pressure: the standard atmosphere climbs 542.3 m from the pressure's pad sample to the one of lowest pressure. Over the same span the column climbs 542.3 meters",
    "`GPS MSL` kept as written, in a unit `Pressure MSL` doesn't settle: over 4 GPS samples within 1 s of one of it, the median difference is 25.0 m read in meters and -166.1 m in feet, so the 300 m a day's weather moves a standard-atmosphere altitude doesn't tell the units apart",
    "`temperature` read in degrees Celsius, from the heights' unit and the manual, p33: the heights are in meters, decided from the pressure. The software sets one unit system for all its displays (p33), here metric, its sensor reading °C or °F (p25). The first value, 18.75, is a bay's temperature in degrees Celsius, -40 to 60",
    "`comp. bat. voltage` read in volts, from the values: they lie within 4.11 to 4.11. That is inside the 0 to 20 V of a battery or an output line. The computer's battery is 3.5 to 8.4 V and the ejection battery at most 10 V (manual p10). Millivolts would be thousands",
    "`lat` and `long` read in degrees, from the values: decimal numbers, as the manual's sample export writes them (Table 2, p40)",
    "`SVs` kept as written, a count, from the manual: the satellites used in the GPS's solution (p25)",
    "`RSSI` kept as written, in dBm, from the manual: the strength of the radio signal the AIM BASE received, \"in decibels (dBm)\" (p29). It is a power level in decibels against a milliwatt, not an SI unit. The record has no quantity for a signal's strength",
    "`GPS Time of Week` kept as written, in milliseconds, from the values: they stay below a week's 604,800,000. They rise by a median of 1000.0 a second of the clock. The record has no quantity for a time of the GPS week",
    "`launch detect`, `apogee detect`, `A cont.`, `B cont.`, `C cont.`, `D cont.`, `A on.`, `B on`, `C on` and `D on` kept as written, flags, from the values: each is 0 or 1, the AIM's flight event status and its lines' continuity (manual p2, p36)",
    "10 rows, the first on line 6, write the run-out GPS group, `time,lat,…`, one empty cell too wide. The cell after it was dropped, so that the groups to its right are read under their names",
    "the file holds `RSSI`, the radio's strength at the AIM BASE. It is the telemetry the ground station received, at the radio's rates, not the flight computer's own log"
  ]
